    "narrative": "For August 14, **Lawrence Butler** (73.95) and **Jonathan Aranda** (68.94) anchor the LawDog lineup as our clear strongest plays. **Lawrence Butler** benefits from a high-ceiling matchup against Rocker, where a friendly park factor and a +13% dynamic platoon boost elevate his utility role into a high-tier start. Similarly, **Jonathan Aranda** is positioned for a productive night against Bassitt; his score is buoyed significantly by a +19% SP skill advantage and an ideal spot in the two-hole, mitigating the concerns typically associated with mid-tier starting pitching matchups. **Gleyber Torres** also warrants attention, as his lead-off spot against a lefty in Newcomb provides the necessary volume and platoon edge to push his score to 59.88, making him a high-floor asset for today\u2019s 5x5 rotation.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Braden Montgomery**, who narrowly missed the starting cut with a score of 39.17. While he benefited from a park factor boost, a -17% penalty against Jobe\u2019s SP profile forced him below our 40-point \"Zebras Floor\" threshold for active starts. We are prioritizing the higher scoring floors of **Brendan Donovan** and **Daulton Varsho**, whose platoon edges against right-handed starters carry more reliability than the volatility seen in the Montgomery matchup. We are also keeping a close watch on **Gavin Sheets**, whose score of 40.12 puts him right on the bubble; his aggressive -19% SP skill penalty against Williams makes him a high-risk play that we are monitoring closely for any late-breaking lineup changes or weather developments in that stadium.\n\nAs we look toward the later games, we must account for the scarcity of stolen base threats in our current configuration. While we are playing **Masyn Winn** for his defensive utility and reliable lineup spot, his proximity to elite hold pitching (53% SB suppression rate) lowers his scoring ceiling to 53.18. We remain cautious with **Jung Hoo Lee** 